<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import ExportListing from './lib/ExportListing.abide'
import exportListingSource from './lib/ExportListing.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import exportContactsSource from './exportContacts.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type Clients = { ui?: boolean; mcp?: boolean; cli?: boolean; openapi?: boolean }`} />

    <Code language="ts" caption="src/admin/rebuildSearchIndex.ts — excerpt" code={`export default POST(rebuild, {
    clients: { ui: false, mcp: false },
})`} />

    <Section title="Every key defaults to true">
        <p>A handler file is on every surface unless its <code>clients</code> says otherwise
        (20.2). That is the right default because the alternative — opting each handler into each
        surface — is four decisions per handler and a fifth surface later that nothing is on.</p>

        <p>Every surface is <strong>derived from the route table</strong> rather than authored
        beside it, so a handler added is a handler listed, and there is no second file to keep in
        step.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A private handler is on no surface">
        <p>A handler a <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> declares is not listed on OpenAPI, on MCP
        or by the CLI, whatever its <code>clients</code> says (16.71). Its file exports nothing
        (34.17), so no other page can import it either: the one caller is the file that declared
        it. So <code>clients</code> is a handler file's option, and the first decision is which of
        the two to write: a handler only one file calls stays in that file, and one meant for
        anything else is a handler file.</p>

        <p>Off every surface is still not off the network. A private handler answers http at its
        address below <code>/__abide/handlers</code>, under the rungs filed over its file
        (16.72).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`clients` decides what is generated, never who may call">
        <Example
            id="clients-listing"
            title={'`clients: { mcp: false }` — off the tool list, on the network'}
            summary="The contact export behind a button, kept out of a model's tool list. Ask the MCP endpoint, then the export's own address: it is listed nowhere a model looks, and it still answers."
            files={{
                'exportContacts.ts': exportContactsSource,
                'lib/ExportListing.abide': exportListingSource,
            }}
        >
            <ExportListing />
        </Example>

        <p><strong><code>clients</code> is not access control.</strong> A handler with <code>mcp:
        false</code> is absent from the tool list and answers on its http address exactly as it did.
        Nothing about withholding a listing withholds the endpoint.</p>

        <p>That separation is deliberate and worth stating plainly, because the shape invites the
        other reading. A listing is a <strong>catalog</strong>; who may call is a
        <strong>rung</strong>. An app that treated a missing catalog entry as a lock would have
        security that depends on nobody guessing an address, which is the failure mode this design
        refuses to make available.</p>

        <p>The rule falls out of it: a handler whose own middleware would refuse a caller is
        <strong>still listed</strong>, and still refused on the call. The catalog describes the
        app, and the rung answers the question.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`ui: false` is the one that reaches the compiler">
        <p><code>ui: false</code> generates no client wrapper, and importing that handler into a
        page is then a <strong>compile error</strong>. That is the difference between it and the
        other three: the others withhold a listing, and this one withholds a name.</p>

        <p>It is what an admin-only mutation wants. The compile error is not a security boundary,
        and the rung is still the answer. But a handler no page should call is a handler no page
        should be able to <em>name</em>, and finding that out at build beats finding it out in
        review.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `Response` turns two of them off by default">
        <p><code>clients.mcp</code> and <code>clients.cli</code> default to <strong>false</strong>
        on a handler that returns a whole <code>Response</code>, a <code>Response</code> having no
        output schema to publish. Turn them back on where the body is JSON you wanted a header
        on.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response
        types</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/machines/describe-your-api-without-writing-a-spec')}>OpenAPI</a> —
            the surface <code>openapi</code> gates</li>
            <li><a href={url('/machines/let-a-model-call-your-handlers')}>MCP</a> — the surface
            <code>mcp</code> gates</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — where access
            control actually goes</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
